DP  - 2010 Jul-Aug
TI  - [Attention deficit hyperactivity disorder (ADHD) in children: neurobiological 
      aspects, diagnosis and therapeutic approach].
PG  - 689-96
AB  - INTRODUCTION: The attention deficit hyperactivity disorder (ADHD) is usually 
      associated to child development, presenting a strong neurobiological influence 
      and being, thus, mostly common in childhood. This disorder is characterized by a 
      persistent pattern of lack of attention and/or by hyperactivity and impulsivity. 
      OBJECTIVE: To conduct a systematic literature review on ADHD in children, 
      focusing neurobiological aspects, diagnosis and therapeutic approaches. 
      METHODOLOGY: This is a literature review about ADHD in childhood, from 1994 
      through 2008. The neurobiological mechanisms that participate in ADHD are 
      complex, involving the dopaminergic, noradrenergic and serotonergic systems. The 
      diagnosis of ADHD must be based on the clinical behavior, since there is no 
      biological marker defined. Metilfenidate is the main drug used for children's 
      treatment. CONCLUSION: The search for relief of ADHD symptoms ought to be based 
      on organic treatments, other specific treatments using medications or 
      neurofeedbacks, as well as behavior therapies.
