TI  - ADHD in adults: a concept in evolution.
PG  - 53-62
LID - 10.1007/s12402-012-0077-3 [doi]
AB  - Although attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder (ADHD) has been recognized as a 
      disorder affecting individuals across the life cycle since the end of the 
      nineties, there is still considerable debate on how to conceptualize the disorder 
      in adults, and on the best way to operationalize diagnostic criteria for this age 
      range. In this comprehensive non-systematic review of the literature, we provide 
      data about prevalence and presentation of ADHD in adulthood as well as discuss 
      major problems in applying criteria developed for children in assessing adults 
      (clinical utility, threshold of symptoms for diagnosis, full ADHD diagnosis in 
      childhood, information source, and additional dimensions for diagnosis-executive 
      functioning impairment and emotional impulsivity). In addition, we provide some 
      recommendations for improving ADHD diagnostic criteria in adulthood.
