TI  - [Mindfulness-based intervention in attention-deficit-/hyperactivity disorder 
      (ADHD)].
PG  - 123-31
LID - 10.1024/1422-4917/a000341 [doi]
AB  - This paper reviews the current literature on mindfulness-based interventions in 
      the treatment of attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder (ADHD). Mindfulness 
      means paying attention and being aware of the experiences occurring in the 
      present moment, and it is usually developed by the practice of meditation. 
      Research shows that mindfulness training is associated with improved attention 
      systems and self-regulation, and that it therefore fosters those skills that are 
      underdeveloped in individuals with ADHD. Although only few studies have 
      investigated the effectiveness of mindfulness training in ADHD (many of which 
      showing methodological limitations), the findings do suggest that mindfulness may 
      be useful in ADHD interventions.
