TI  - Feigned ADHD Associated Cognitive Impairment: Utility of Integrating an 
      Eye-tracker and the MOXO-dCPT.
PG  - 1212-1222
LID - 10.1177/10870547211063643 [doi]
AB  - OBJECTIVE: The current study assessed the utility of eye-movements measures, 
      gathered while participants performed a commercially available Continuous 
      Performance Test (CPT), to detect feigned ADHD-associated cognitive impairment. 
      METHOD: Healthy simulators (n = 37), ADHD patients (n = 33), and healthy controls 
      (n = 36) performed an eye-tracker integrated MOXO-dCPT and a stand-alone validity 
      indicator. RESULTS: Simulators gazed significantly longer at regions that were 
      irrelevant for successful MOXO-dCPT performance compared to ADHD patients and 
      healthy controls. This eye-movement measure, however, had lower sensitivity than 
      traditional MOXO-dCPT indices. DISCUSSION: Gaze direction measures, gathered 
      while performing a CPT, show initial promise as validity indicators. Traditional 
      CPT measures, however, are more sensitive and therefore offer a more promising 
      path for the establishment of CPT-based validity indicators. The current study is 
      an initial exploration of the issue and further evaluation of both theoretical 
      and practical aspects is mandated.
